FF&E — furniture, fixtures and equipment — is the layer of a hotel that guests actually
touch. Getting it delivered on time and on spec is less about any single decision and more
about a sequence of them, each protecting the one after it.

## Start with the specification, not the catalogue

The single biggest source of delay is a spec that is approved twice — once on paper, once
again when the physical sample doesn't match what everyone pictured. Locking a finish
against a physical sample before it is ordered at scale removes almost all of the rework
that otherwise surfaces on site, months later, when it is expensive to fix.

## Lead time is a portfolio problem, not a per-item one

A single chair might have an eight-week lead time. Two hundred and forty of them, finished
to match an existing property, across three factories, is a different problem — and the
schedule has to be built around the slowest input, not the average one.

## Quality control belongs at the factory, not the loading dock

Catching a finish inconsistency after a container has landed means either accepting it or
re-making it on an impossible timeline. In-house QC at the point of manufacture is what
makes "right first time" achievable rather than aspirational.

## What good procurement actually delivers

Not the lowest unit price — the lowest total cost of ownership, including the freight, the
duty, the rework that didn't happen, and the property that still looks the way it was
designed to look on its tenth anniversary.
